1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,013
Welcome to Code Quest,
Chapter 2: Values and Variables.

2
00:00:04,013 --> 00:00:08,430
In this chapter, you will
learn how a program stores data,

3
00:00:08,430 --> 00:00:11,642
gives it a name and calculates with it.

4
00:00:12,417 --> 00:00:13,595
First, a puzzle.

5
00:00:13,595 --> 00:00:17,523
A learner asked an AI
assistant for the average of

6
00:00:17,523 --> 00:00:20,272
three test scores: 80, 90 and 100.

7
00:00:20,272 --> 00:00:22,236
The AI wrote this code,

8
00:00:22,236 --> 00:00:25,378
and it said the program would print 90.

9
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:28,113
Look closely at line 5.

10
00:00:28,113 --> 00:00:30,076
Do you trust the prediction?

11
00:00:34,850 --> 00:00:38,206
Here is the real output: 203 point 3 3.

12
00:00:38,206 --> 00:00:41,188
The AI was sure, and it was wrong.

13
00:00:41,188 --> 00:00:43,426
By the end of this chapter,

14
00:00:43,426 --> 00:00:46,035
you will see why in two seconds.

15
00:00:47,067 --> 00:00:51,231
Here is what you will be
able to do by the end.

16
00:00:51,231 --> 00:00:53,313
First, write variables with clear names,

17
00:00:53,313 --> 00:00:56,089
and give them values with the equals sign.

18
00:00:56,850 --> 00:00:59,975
Second, explain the four
basic types of data,

19
00:00:59,975 --> 00:01:02,709
and check the type of any value.

20
00:01:02,709 --> 00:01:05,443
Third, calculate with
the seven arithmetic operators.

21
00:01:06,217 --> 00:01:10,387
Fourth, predict the output of
a program before you run it.

22
00:01:10,387 --> 00:01:14,937
And fifth, fix the errors that
beginners meet most often with variables.

23
00:01:15,967 --> 00:01:19,573
In Chapter 1, the cafe
receipt typed each price twice:

24
00:01:19,573 --> 00:01:23,179
once in the item line,
and once in the total.

25
00:01:23,179 --> 00:01:25,343
If the price of tea changes,

26
00:01:25,343 --> 00:01:27,867
you must find and change every copy.

27
00:01:27,867 --> 00:01:30,391
Miss one, and the receipt is wrong.

28
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:32,556
A variable solves this.

29
00:01:32,556 --> 00:01:36,070
A variable is a name
that refers to a value.

30
00:01:36,070 --> 00:01:40,286
You store the price once, under
a name such as tea price,

31
00:01:40,286 --> 00:01:42,044
and use that name everywhere.

32
00:01:42,817 --> 00:01:44,997
Every real program works this way.

33
00:01:44,997 --> 00:01:48,266
A shopping app keeps
the items in your basket.

34
00:01:48,266 --> 00:01:50,083
A game keeps your score.

35
00:01:50,083 --> 00:01:52,263
A banking app keeps your balance.

36
00:01:52,263 --> 00:01:55,896
Each of these values has
a name inside the program.

37
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:00,510
A value is a single
piece of data, such as 3,

38
00:02:00,510 --> 00:02:02,152
3.5 or the word Tea.

39
00:02:02,152 --> 00:02:03,793
Every value has a type.

40
00:02:03,793 --> 00:02:07,075
The type tells Python what
kind of data it is,

41
00:02:07,075 --> 00:02:09,372
and what you can do with it.

42
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:11,939
Python has four basic types.

43
00:02:11,939 --> 00:02:14,086
An int is a whole number.

44
00:02:14,086 --> 00:02:17,306
A float is a number with a decimal point.

45
00:02:17,306 --> 00:02:19,810
A string is text inside quotation marks.

46
00:02:19,810 --> 00:02:22,672
And a Boolean is either True or False.

47
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:25,754
Assignment connects a name to a value.

48
00:02:25,754 --> 00:02:29,374
You write the name, then
the equals sign, then the value.

49
00:02:29,374 --> 00:02:31,677
Python works out the right side first.

50
00:02:31,677 --> 00:02:35,297
Then it connects the name
on the left to the result.

51
00:02:36,050 --> 00:02:38,752
So read the equals sign as now refers to,

52
00:02:38,752 --> 00:02:40,253
not as is equal to.

53
00:02:40,253 --> 00:02:43,255
Think of a variable as
a name label that you

54
00:02:43,255 --> 00:02:44,456
stick on a value.

55
00:02:44,456 --> 00:02:48,058
You can move the label to
a new value at any time.

56
00:02:48,817 --> 00:02:51,255
The comparison stops working at one point.

57
00:02:51,255 --> 00:02:54,042
Two labels can point to the same value,

58
00:02:54,042 --> 00:02:57,178
and moving one label
does not move the other.

59
00:02:58,200 --> 00:02:58,965
Step 1.

60
00:02:58,965 --> 00:03:02,410
Create a file called
ex 0 1 first variables.

61
00:03:02,410 --> 00:03:06,619
Line 2 stores the text
Asha in a variable called customer.

62
00:03:06,619 --> 00:03:10,063
Line 3 stores the number 3 in tea price.

63
00:03:10,063 --> 00:03:12,359
The next three lines print them.

64
00:03:13,117 --> 00:03:16,414
Before you run it, predict
the three lines of output.

65
00:03:22,183 --> 00:03:23,765
Here is the output.

66
00:03:23,765 --> 00:03:27,720
Asha, then Tea costs 3,
then Two teas cost 6.

67
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:30,093
In the print on line 4,

68
00:03:30,093 --> 00:03:32,861
the name customer has no quotation marks.

69
00:03:32,861 --> 00:03:37,212
So Python prints its value,
Asha, and not the word customer.

70
00:03:37,983 --> 00:03:40,914
Good names make code easy to read.

71
00:03:40,914 --> 00:03:45,520
The Python style guide, called
PEP 8, says: use lowercase letters,

72
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:48,032
and join words with an underscore.

73
00:03:48,032 --> 00:03:51,381
A name can contain
letters, digits and underscores,

74
00:03:51,381 --> 00:03:54,312
but it cannot start with a digit.

75
00:03:55,083 --> 00:03:56,111
Capital letters matter,

76
00:03:56,111 --> 00:03:59,539
so price with a small p and Price with a

77
00:03:59,539 --> 00:04:01,595
capital P are two different names.

78
00:04:01,595 --> 00:04:05,022
And do not use the
words Python reserves for itself,

79
00:04:05,022 --> 00:04:07,078
such as True, None or class.

80
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:08,838
Step 2.

81
00:04:08,838 --> 00:04:12,525
The type function tells you
the type of any value.

82
00:04:12,525 --> 00:04:16,582
This program stores four values:
a count of cups, a price,

83
00:04:16,582 --> 00:04:19,901
an item name and a yes or no fact.

84
00:04:19,901 --> 00:04:22,851
Then it prints each value with its type.

85
00:04:23,617 --> 00:04:24,335
Run it.

86
00:04:24,335 --> 00:04:26,132
The 2 is an int.

87
00:04:26,132 --> 00:04:27,928
The 3.5 is a float.

88
00:04:27,928 --> 00:04:31,161
Tea is a string, shown as s t r.

89
00:04:31,161 --> 00:04:32,958
And True is a bool.

90
00:04:33,717 --> 00:04:37,496
Python chose each type from
the way you wrote the value.

91
00:04:37,496 --> 00:04:41,618
A decimal point makes a float,
and quotation marks make a string.

92
00:04:41,618 --> 00:04:44,366
True and False need
a capital first letter.

93
00:04:45,367 --> 00:04:46,294
Step 3.

94
00:04:46,294 --> 00:04:50,467
An operator is a
symbol that does a calculation.

95
00:04:50,467 --> 00:04:53,713
Seventeen biscuits are
shared between five friends.

96
00:04:53,713 --> 00:04:56,958
This program uses
all seven arithmetic operators.

97
00:04:57,717 --> 00:05:00,709
Pause the video and predict each line.

98
00:05:00,709 --> 00:05:04,557
Pay special attention to
divide, floor divide and remainder.

99
00:05:13,317 --> 00:05:15,560
Here are the results.

100
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:21,169
Add gives 22, subtract gives
12, and multiply gives 85.

101
00:05:21,169 --> 00:05:22,852
Divide gives 3.4.

102
00:05:22,852 --> 00:05:25,095
Floor divide gives 3.

103
00:05:25,095 --> 00:05:26,778
Remainder gives 2.

104
00:05:26,778 --> 00:05:29,022
And power gives 25.

105
00:05:29,783 --> 00:05:31,943
Three operators often surprise beginners.

106
00:05:31,943 --> 00:05:34,103
A single slash is division.

107
00:05:34,103 --> 00:05:36,695
It gives the exact answer, 3.4,

108
00:05:36,695 --> 00:05:39,719
and the result is always a float.

109
00:05:39,719 --> 00:05:42,311
A double slash is floor division.

110
00:05:42,311 --> 00:05:47,495
It gives the whole part, so
each friend gets 3 whole biscuits.

111
00:05:48,250 --> 00:05:52,632
The percent sign gives the remainder,
so 2 biscuits are left over.

112
00:05:52,632 --> 00:05:55,918
Finally, two stars raise
a number to a power.

113
00:05:55,918 --> 00:05:59,569
So 5 to the power 2 is 5 times 5,

114
00:05:59,569 --> 00:06:00,664
which is 25.

115
00:06:01,667 --> 00:06:02,568
Step 4.

116
00:06:02,568 --> 00:06:05,721
A variable can get a new value.

117
00:06:05,721 --> 00:06:07,973
Here, points starts at 10.

118
00:06:07,973 --> 00:06:11,577
Line 4 says: points equals points plus 5.

119
00:06:12,350 --> 00:06:15,297
In mathematics, that
line would be impossible.

120
00:06:15,297 --> 00:06:20,349
In Python, it means: take the
current value of points, add 5,

121
00:06:20,349 --> 00:06:22,875
and give points the new value.

122
00:06:22,875 --> 00:06:25,823
Python works out the right side first.

123
00:06:26,583 --> 00:06:30,315
So the output shows Start
10, After a quiz 15,

124
00:06:30,315 --> 00:06:32,181
and After the bonus 30.

125
00:06:32,181 --> 00:06:34,793
Line 6 doubled the value of 15.

126
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:36,793
Step 5.

127
00:06:36,793 --> 00:06:40,269
Python follows the
same order as mathematics.

128
00:06:40,269 --> 00:06:42,255
It calculates powers first.

129
00:06:42,255 --> 00:06:44,738
Then it multiplies and divides.

130
00:06:44,738 --> 00:06:47,220
Then it adds and subtracts.

131
00:06:47,220 --> 00:06:49,206
Brackets change the order.

132
00:06:49,983 --> 00:06:52,654
Predict lines 2 and 3 before you look.

133
00:06:58,417 --> 00:07:02,179
Line 2 multiplies first,
so the result is 14.

134
00:07:02,179 --> 00:07:07,196
Line 3 adds first because of
the brackets, so it gives 20.

135
00:07:07,196 --> 00:07:10,958
Line 4 divides first,
and gives 8.0, a float,

136
00:07:10,958 --> 00:07:13,467
because division always gives a float.

137
00:07:14,217 --> 00:07:16,147
Line 5 is a surprise.

138
00:07:16,147 --> 00:07:20,007
Point 1 plus point 1 plus point 1 does not

139
00:07:20,007 --> 00:07:21,551
print exactly point 3.

140
00:07:21,551 --> 00:07:25,025
Computers store decimal numbers
in binary, so some values,

141
00:07:25,025 --> 00:07:28,113
such as point 1, carry a tiny error.

142
00:07:28,113 --> 00:07:29,271
On line 6,

143
00:07:29,271 --> 00:07:33,132
the round function rounds the
result to 2 decimal places,

144
00:07:33,132 --> 00:07:35,062
and you get point 3.

145
00:07:36,067 --> 00:07:40,451
Here is the cafe receipt
from Chapter 1, rebuilt with variables.

146
00:07:40,451 --> 00:07:44,836
Lines 2 to 7 store
the inputs: the customer, two prices,

147
00:07:44,836 --> 00:07:49,619
the number of teas, a member
flag and a 10 percent discount.

148
00:07:50,383 --> 00:07:54,263
Lines 9 to 12 calculate
the tea total, the bill,

149
00:07:54,263 --> 00:07:56,979
the discount and the amount to pay.

150
00:07:56,979 --> 00:08:01,635
Each calculation uses names, not numbers,
so every price appears only once.

151
00:08:02,417 --> 00:08:03,345
Run it.

152
00:08:03,345 --> 00:08:06,130
Two teas at 3.5 cost 7.0.

153
00:08:06,130 --> 00:08:11,701
The bill is 13.0, the discount
is 1.3, and Asha pays 11.7.

154
00:08:11,701 --> 00:08:14,486
Notice that 7.0 is a float,

155
00:08:14,486 --> 00:08:18,200
because one of its parts was a float.

156
00:08:18,950 --> 00:08:22,518
Now change tea price to
4 and run it again.

157
00:08:22,518 --> 00:08:26,442
Every line that depends on
the tea price updates by itself.

158
00:08:26,442 --> 00:08:28,939
For now, the discount applies every time.

159
00:08:28,939 --> 00:08:32,864
In Chapter 6, you will
use the member flag to decide.

160
00:08:38,867 --> 00:08:41,782
The AI skill for
this chapter is prediction.

161
00:08:41,782 --> 00:08:43,604
Before you run a program,

162
00:08:43,604 --> 00:08:47,249
ask an AI assistant such
as ChatGPT or Claude to

163
00:08:47,249 --> 00:08:48,342
predict its output.

164
00:08:48,342 --> 00:08:50,529
Write down your own prediction too.

165
00:08:50,529 --> 00:08:51,987
Then run the code.

166
00:08:51,987 --> 00:08:55,267
The real output is
the only answer that counts.

167
00:08:56,017 --> 00:08:59,451
The safety habits from
Chapter 1 still apply.

168
00:08:59,451 --> 00:09:02,457
Never paste passwords,
keys or private data.

169
00:09:02,457 --> 00:09:05,462
Run every piece of AI code yourself.

170
00:09:05,462 --> 00:09:08,897
You are responsible for the code you use.

171
00:09:09,650 --> 00:09:12,017
AI helps at every stage.

172
00:09:12,017 --> 00:09:14,384
Plan the variables you need.

173
00:09:14,384 --> 00:09:17,698
Write the code
yourself, then compare predictions.

174
00:09:17,698 --> 00:09:20,064
Generate examples with fixed names.

175
00:09:20,064 --> 00:09:22,431
Debug with the full error.

176
00:09:22,431 --> 00:09:24,325
Test with new values.

177
00:09:24,325 --> 00:09:26,692
And improve your variable names.

178
00:09:27,450 --> 00:09:29,461
Here is the Write card.

179
00:09:29,461 --> 00:09:30,668
Paste your code,

180
00:09:30,668 --> 00:09:35,494
and ask the AI to predict
the exact output line by line,

181
00:09:35,494 --> 00:09:37,505
without running or changing it.

182
00:09:37,505 --> 00:09:39,918
Then compare three
things: your prediction,

183
00:09:39,918 --> 00:09:42,734
the AI's prediction and the real output.

184
00:09:43,517 --> 00:09:45,494
And here is the Debug card.

185
00:09:45,494 --> 00:09:47,801
Give the AI the exact error message,

186
00:09:47,801 --> 00:09:50,438
and ask for the cause before the fix.

187
00:09:51,217 --> 00:09:53,460
Now, the AI pair moment.

188
00:09:53,460 --> 00:09:56,151
Remember the puzzle from the start?

189
00:09:56,151 --> 00:09:59,740
The AI wrote this
average, and predicted 90.

190
00:09:59,740 --> 00:10:02,431
It printed 203 point 3 3.

191
00:10:02,431 --> 00:10:02,880
Why?

192
00:10:03,650 --> 00:10:05,833
Use what you learned in step 5.

193
00:10:12,583 --> 00:10:15,369
The AI forgot the order of operations.

194
00:10:15,369 --> 00:10:18,155
Python divided only score 3 by 3,

195
00:10:18,155 --> 00:10:20,941
and then added the other two scores.

196
00:10:20,941 --> 00:10:25,718
Brackets around the sum fix the
order, and the program prints 90.0.

197
00:10:26,483 --> 00:10:29,395
A quick check would also catch this flaw.

198
00:10:29,395 --> 00:10:33,036
An average of three scores
must be between the lowest

199
00:10:33,036 --> 00:10:34,492
and the highest score.

200
00:10:34,492 --> 00:10:36,676
A result of 203 is impossible.

201
00:10:36,676 --> 00:10:40,316
Code that runs is not
always code that is correct.

202
00:10:41,333 --> 00:10:42,881
Now, four common mistakes.

203
00:10:42,881 --> 00:10:46,748
Mistake 1 is a typing
error in a variable name.

204
00:10:46,748 --> 00:10:50,230
Line 4 uses tea prise, spelled with an s.

205
00:10:50,230 --> 00:10:52,937
Python only knows the names you created,

206
00:10:52,937 --> 00:10:55,645
so it stops with a Name Error.

207
00:10:55,645 --> 00:10:57,966
It even suggests the right name.

208
00:10:58,717 --> 00:11:01,367
Mistake 2 is a number stored as text.

209
00:11:01,367 --> 00:11:04,680
With quotation marks, 3 is
a string, not an int.

210
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:07,330
Python will not add text and a number,

211
00:11:07,330 --> 00:11:09,318
so you get a Type Error.

212
00:11:09,318 --> 00:11:11,969
The fix is to remove the quotation marks.

213
00:11:12,750 --> 00:11:16,809
Sometimes this mistake gives no
error at all, which is worse.

214
00:11:16,809 --> 00:11:20,129
Multiplying the string 3
by 2 repeats the text,

215
00:11:20,129 --> 00:11:22,712
so you get 33 instead of 6.

216
00:11:22,712 --> 00:11:25,664
If a result looks strange, check the type.

217
00:11:26,417 --> 00:11:29,470
Mistake 3 is a name
that starts with a digit.

218
00:11:29,470 --> 00:11:32,523
Python reads the 2 as
the start of a number,

219
00:11:32,523 --> 00:11:34,965
and does not understand
the letters after it.

220
00:11:34,965 --> 00:11:38,018
Start the name with a
letter, such as second place.

221
00:11:38,783 --> 00:11:42,719
Mistake 4 puts the name and
the value on the wrong sides.

222
00:11:42,719 --> 00:11:45,999
A literal is a value
written directly in the code,

223
00:11:45,999 --> 00:11:49,278
such as 10, and it cannot get a new value.

224
00:11:49,278 --> 00:11:52,558
The name always goes on
the left: points equals 10.

225
00:11:53,317 --> 00:11:55,633
The habit from Chapter 1 still works.

226
00:11:55,633 --> 00:11:58,280
Read the last line of an error first.

227
00:11:58,280 --> 00:12:00,265
It names the kind of error.

228
00:12:00,265 --> 00:12:02,250
Then look at the line number.

229
00:12:03,267 --> 00:12:05,668
Two questions to
think beyond this chapter.

230
00:12:05,668 --> 00:12:08,413
First: four friends share a bill of 50.

231
00:12:08,413 --> 00:12:11,843
How could you use floor
division and remainder to find

232
00:12:11,843 --> 00:12:14,588
how much each person pays in whole units,

233
00:12:14,588 --> 00:12:16,646
and how much is left over?

234
00:12:23,417 --> 00:12:26,401
Second: a is 5 and b is 8,

235
00:12:26,401 --> 00:12:28,640
and you want to swap them.

236
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:33,117
Why does a equals b, followed
by b equals a, not work?

237
00:12:33,117 --> 00:12:34,983
Predict the result, test it,

238
00:12:34,983 --> 00:12:38,341
then find a fix
that uses a third variable.

239
00:12:45,367 --> 00:12:46,031
Let's recap.

240
00:12:46,031 --> 00:12:48,356
A value is a piece of data,

241
00:12:48,356 --> 00:12:52,011
and every value has a
type: int, float, string or bool.

242
00:12:52,011 --> 00:12:55,333
A variable is a name
that refers to a value.

243
00:12:55,333 --> 00:12:58,654
The equals sign connects the
name on the left to

244
00:12:58,654 --> 00:13:00,315
the value on the right.

245
00:13:01,083 --> 00:13:03,980
Python works out the right side first.

246
00:13:03,980 --> 00:13:06,464
Single slash always gives a float.

247
00:13:06,464 --> 00:13:11,016
Double slash gives the whole
part, and percent gives the remainder.

248
00:13:11,016 --> 00:13:13,499
Brackets control the order of operations.

249
00:13:14,250 --> 00:13:18,037
And with AI: ask it
to predict, make your own prediction,

250
00:13:18,037 --> 00:13:20,103
and let the real run decide.

251
00:13:20,883 --> 00:13:24,041
You earned 100 XP
and the Name Keeper badge.

252
00:13:25,067 --> 00:13:25,915
Now practise.

253
00:13:25,915 --> 00:13:29,308
There are eight tasks
in the practice file.

254
00:13:29,308 --> 00:13:34,398
The three warm-up tasks are worth
10 XP each: store and print,

255
00:13:34,398 --> 00:13:37,367
